        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Workspace Semantic Presentation\n\n**Status: proposed experiment**\n\nThe current runtime proves that a graph can replace its internal rendering with a graph-level view. This draft refines when that happens, which authored view appears, and how graph authoring should control the result.\n\nThe proposal is deliberately reviewable rather than normative."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-semantic-presentation-declaration",
          "nodeId": "workspace-semantic-presentation-landing-surface"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-problem",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "What feels wrong",
      "position": {
        "x": -1180,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 340,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## What feels wrong\n\nThe current experiment first selects Detail at the old dot threshold. By then, the projected graph has too few screen pixels for Detail to remain readable. The recursive idea is sound, but the presentation band and rendering path are wrong."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-rule",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Proposed recursive rule",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Proposed recursive rule\n\nA graph participating in a workspace is a node-like projection of its declaration.\n\n- Close: render expanded graph contents.\n- Medium: render the exposed Detail View.\n- Far: render the exposed Summary View.\n- Farther: render the exposed Icon or Glyph.\n- Extreme: use a dot only if the workspace still needs one.\n\nDetail replaces the expanded graph while Detail is still readable; it does not begin at the dot boundary."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-selection",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "How a level is selected",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Selection should use projected size\n\nChoose the graph presentation from its resulting screen footprint, with zoom as an input rather than the whole rule. A large graph and a small graph may cross bands at different camera zoom values.\n\nUse hysteresis around every boundary so cards do not flicker while the user zooms. Keep authored relative semantic scale as an optional offset."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-authoring",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Authoring contract",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## The declaration authors the outside view\n\nThe declaration exposes Detail, Summary, Icon, Glyph, and Landing surfaces. Those are the graph’s authored representations when another workspace treats the graph as a node.\n\nEditing graph contents defines the inside. Editing exposed views defines how the graph appears from outside at each scale. Current new-graph defaults provide a complete publishable fallback."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-rendering",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Rendering contract",
      "position": {
        "x": -1180,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Render the real view\n\nWorkspace presentation should use the ordinary view renderer, including the authored payload, style, dimensions, and delegation rules. A special compact text preview is insufficient.\n\nOnce the level leaves Expanded, hide the graph’s internal node, edge, group, handle, and interface layers and render exactly one exposed representation."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-geometry",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Geometry and interfaces",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Geometry follows the chosen representation\n\nSelection, minimap regions, workspace edges, bridge apertures, portal routes, marquee hit testing, and collision placement use the selected presentation bounds.\n\nPorts, portals, and bridges remain stable workspace interfaces. Semantic presentation changes the graph content projection; it must not erase or silently move its capabilities."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-editing",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Proposed authoring experience",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Authoring experience\n\nThe graph author can preview Expanded, Detail, Summary, Icon, and Glyph without manipulating the whole workspace camera. The declaration editor shows which surface owns each level and whether a fallback is being used.\n\nA newly created graph begins with all standard views. Authors may replace their content or delegate them to graph-owned nodes."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-implementation",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Implementation slices",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Proposed implementation slices\n\n1. Replace the collapsed boolean with an explicit workspace presentation level.\n2. Resolve levels from projected screen bounds with hysteresis.\n3. Render the exposed view through the normal view pipeline.\n4. Route all workspace geometry through presentation bounds.\n5. Add declaration-level preview and exposure controls.\n6. Add recursive workspace smoke fixtures."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-tests",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Reviewable smoke contract",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Smoke contract\n\nOpen several current-standard graphs with distinct Detail, Summary, Icon, and Glyph content. Zoom across each boundary in both directions. Verify:\n\n- the right authored view appears at each band;\n- no internal dot field remains beneath a graph-level view;\n- the focused graph participates;\n- interfaces and typed workspace edges remain connected;\n- semantic zoom Off keeps graphs expanded;\n- transitions do not flicker."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-questions",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Questions for review",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Questions for review\n\n- Which minimum screen dimensions make Detail, Summary, and Icon useful?\n- Should authors adjust thresholds per graph, or only set a relative semantic baseline?\n- Is Glyph the terminal workspace representation, or do we retain Dot for extremely large workspaces?\n- Should selecting a collapsed graph promote it, or should only zoom change its presentation?"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-checkpoint",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Review checkpoint",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "markdown": "## Review checkpoint\n\nThis proposal is ready to implement when we agree that:\n\n1. Graph-level semantic presentation is recursive view selection.\n2. Screen readability determines transition points.\n3. Exposed declaration views are authoritative.\n4. Workspace interfaces survive every visual level.\n5. Authoring includes direct preview of every outside representation."
      }
    }
  ],
